WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:10.261
This is Osprey Storage: one app for the Mac and the iPhone. Its logic is written in Osprey, and its platform code in Swift. Here's how the two work together.

00:11.011 --> 00:15.792
The app measures a folder, hidden files included, and ranks what takes up the space.

00:16.545 --> 00:20.281
Open any folder, and it ranks what's inside, all the way down.

00:24.645 --> 00:32.160
The work is split in two. Osprey owns the browser: its state, its events, the ranking, the hidden-file rule, and every screen.

00:32.360 --> 00:38.126
Swift owns what only the platform can do: the window, the folder picker, and the walk over the disk.

00:38.876 --> 00:44.231
Osprey exposes the app as two functions. Start builds the first screen for a home folder.

00:44.431 --> 00:49.668
Dispatch takes the state the last screen came with, and one event, and returns the next screen.

00:50.418 --> 01:04.167
The compiler builds the project for the Mac into a static library, and writes a C header beside it. Every export gets a plain C prototype: Osprey strings become char pointers, and integers become sixty-four bit ints.

01:04.917 --> 01:11.123
Swift includes that header through a bridging header, initializes Osprey once, and asks it for the first screen.

01:11.323 --> 01:17.699
Each tap or toggle goes back through dispatch, with the state Swift was handed, which it keeps as opaque text.

01:18.449 --> 01:24.101
Calls go the other way too. Osprey declares each function it needs from the host with extern.

01:24.301 --> 01:28.138
Storage scan, for example, lists one folder of the measured walk.

01:28.888 --> 01:34.009
The same header lists these as imports, functions the host must define with C linkage.

01:34.209 --> 01:39.086
Here is storage scan's prototype: a C string in, a sixty-four bit int out.

01:39.836 --> 01:45.127
Swift defines it with @_cdecl, so the linker finds exactly that C name and signature.

01:45.327 --> 01:50.013
The body reads the tree Swift measured, and returns how many entries the folder has.

01:50.763 --> 01:56.352
The browser's logic never calls those externs directly. It renders through a Disk Facts effect.

01:56.552 --> 02:00.517
At the boundary, this handler answers that effect with the Swift functions.

02:01.267 --> 02:07.463
That makes the Osprey side testable on its own. The test suite answers the same effect with fixed data.

02:07.663 --> 02:12.824
So the browser and every screen run natively in the tests, with no Swift and no disk.

02:13.574 --> 02:21.787
Each screen Osprey returns is a tree of native nodes. Here, the Hidden Files toggle is a node, with its value and the event it sends.

02:21.987 --> 02:28.244
A shared SwiftUI renderer draws that node as a real toggle, and sends its event straight back to Osprey.

02:28.994 --> 02:32.679
Flip it in the app, and the hidden folders drop out of every total.

02:37.061 --> 02:44.571
One Xcode target builds for the Mac, the iPhone, and the simulator. Its settings name the platform for each SDK.

02:44.771 --> 02:50.416
Then it links the Osprey library built for that platform, and imports its header through the bridging header.

02:51.166 --> 02:58.291
So the same Osprey code runs on iPhone, drawn with the phone's own controls, dark mode and larger text included.

02:59.041 --> 03:11.286
Now run the Osprey side's tests. They answer the Disk Facts effect with fixed data, so the browser runs with no Swift, no window and no disk. This one checks the ranking under the hidden-files setting.

03:11.486 --> 03:16.392
These check every screen against what the disk holds, and what an iPhone lets an app see.

03:16.592 --> 03:18.702
All seven pass, in a few seconds.

03:19.452 --> 03:31.871
Now change the rule that decides what's hidden. In sight says an entry is shown when hidden files are on, or when the entry isn't hidden. Replace the whole body with true, so hidden entries always show.

03:32.071 --> 03:36.667
Counted builds on it, so the totals change too. Nothing in Swift has changed.

03:37.417 --> 03:46.193
Run the tests again. The ranking test fails: hidden entries like Library and dot cache now appear, and the hidden contents are counted in the total.

03:46.393 --> 03:53.117
The screens test fails too. The hidden folder is no longer gone, and the size isn't the visible size any more.

03:53.317 --> 04:05.174
Five pass, two fail, and each failure names the check and the values. The rule lives in one Osprey function, and the tests guard it on both platforms at once. Put it back, and all seven pass.

04:05.924 --> 04:19.259
To try it, run the three commands on screen: the storage target for the Mac, the run script for the iPhone simulator, and the tests, which need no Swift at all. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects.
